<p>NOTE. -- When a word or phrase is preceded by one of the little words <b=
>f, l, rd, rm, rf, rso, rl, rs, mr, mf, fm</b>, these words alone take the =
mark of the feminine or plural; the other words that follow them do not nee=
d to receive this mark. They are perfectly understood.</p>
<div class=3D"c4">EXAMPLES:</div>
<p><i>The stallions,</i> <b>l' frsod;</b><br>
<i>My brothers,</i> <b>rd' rsmr;</b><br>
<i>My sisters are dead,</i> <b>rd'=C2=AF rsmr frm rfmd;</b><br>
<i>These orphans [f] are not unhappy,</i> <b>fm'=C2=AF mdfso d frm sosm.</b=
><br></p>
<p>It is only when the noun is alone, isolated, that the sign of the femini=
ne ( =C2=AF ) or of the plural ( ' ) is necessary on the noun itself.</p>
<div class=3D"c4">EXAMPLES:</div>
<table>
